"Generates strategic options by deliberately matching internal strengths and weaknesses with external opportunities and threats.\n\nUse when: Turn a SWOT assessment into candidate actions. Compare offensive, defensive and capability-building strategic responses.\n\nAvoid when: Internal and external factors have not yet been identified or tested.\n\nVisual grammar: Factor lists form the matrix margins. Four interaction regions contain SO, ST, WO and WT options. Short factor identifiers make each option's reasoning traceable.\n\nBuild: Start with a scoped and evidence-backed SWOT factor set. Create options by matching specific internal and external factors. Assess feasibility and tradeoffs separately before selecting commitments.\n\nCheck: Each option identifies the factors it actually combines. Options describe possible action rather than restating a factor. Weakness-threat responses include realistic protection or withdrawal choices.\n\nPitfalls: Renaming an ordinary four-list SWOT as a TOWS matrix. Assuming a factor combination guarantees a viable strategy.\n\nExample: A small manufacturer uses technical expertise to enter a growing niche and proposes partnerships to address a sales weakness.\n\nAlternatives: SWOT analysis, Weighted decision matrix."
